<Layout title="About - Garret James Hokr" description="A career-switcher's path from bartending to the sports industry.">
  <section class="mx-auto max-w-2xl px-6 py-16">
    <h1 class="font-serif text-4xl font-semibold tracking-tight">Just a kid from New Jersey</h1>
    <p class="mt-3 text-ink-soft">The long way into the sports industry.</p>

    <div class="mt-10 space-y-6 text-ink-soft leading-relaxed">
      <p>
        I was seven years old the first time a World Cup match got me out of bed before sunrise. That morning stuck with me for two decades — but the road from a kid glued to the TV to an adult working inside the sports industry was nowhere near a straight line.
      </p>
      <p>
        Along the way I was a bartender, a barista, a security guard, a travel agent, and a business English trainer in Spain. None of those jobs had "sports" in the title. All of them taught me something I'd eventually need — reading a room, staying calm under pressure, making strangers comfortable, showing up when it wasn't glamorous.
      </p>
      <p>
        At 28, with a good bartending job and steady money in New York City, I made the leap: enrolled at The FBA – The Football Business Academy, paid the tuition in cash by picking up bar shifts six days a week, and moved to Lisbon to start over from zero in an industry I had no formal background in and no professional connections to.
      </p>
      <p>
        Breaking in afterward didn't happen through a perfect resume or a dream job posting. It happened through volunteering at grassroots soccer events, following up after rejections instead of going quiet, and taking a ticketing and inside-sales role at the Colorado Rapids that wasn't the "dream job" on paper but turned out to be the right room. From there, one step led to the next, until I was finally doing the work I'd wanted since I was seven.
      </p>
      <p>
        Today I write about that path — the detours, the rejections, the unglamorous jobs that mattered more than they seemed to at the time — for anyone else trying to change careers or find their way into an industry that doesn't hand out easy entry points.
      </p>
      <p>
        Outside of this newsletter, I'm also building the <a href="https://2631foundation.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="text-outline font-semibold text-gold-dark hover:text-gold-deep transition-colors">2631 Foundation</a>, working to make sure the kids growing up near the 2026 World Cup, the 2028 LA Olympics, and beyond actually get to benefit from the sport — not just watch it happen around them.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>
</Layout>
